==Aftermath==
The continuation of Uribe's [[Democratic security|Democratic Security]] policy led to further movement against the [[National Liberation Army (Colombia)|ELN]] and the [[FARC]], including new anti-terrorist and anti-drug trafficking laws, increasing the power of the police. In 2008, Uribe was able to negotiate the release of [[Íngrid Betancourt|Ingrid Betancourt]], a high-profile politician who was held as one of many hostages by the FARC. Strongly backed and supported financially by the US, the policy continued to combat Colombia's leftist [[Paramilitary|Paramilitary groups]]. The project and its success led to Uribe receiving the [[Presidential Medal of Freedom|US Presidential Medal of Freedom]] in 2009 for his efforts in this regard.<ref name=":14">{{Cite news|url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Alvaro-Uribe-Velez|title=Alvaro Uribe Velez {{!}} president of Colombia|work=Encyclopedia Britannica|access-date=2017-04-09|language=en}}</ref>

===Parapolitical scandal===
Events shortly after the 2006 elections marked the beginning of a years-long process of addressing apparent paramilitary connections in government. In November 2006, a document was discovered connecting various members of Uribe's coalition ("uribistas") and the paramilitary group [[United Self-Defense Forces of Colombia|AUC]], involving negotiations for votes and support. This document, the [[Ralito Agreement]], was signed in a 2001 meeting by representatives of Paramilitary groups, and by 18 current and future politicians, who promised in it to "work toward a new Colombia"<ref name=":7" /> and thus secure power for the Paramilitaries.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://colombiareports.com/colombia-captures-ex-senator-for-paramilitary-ties/|title=Colombia captures ex-senator for paramilitary ties|date=2012-06-26|work=Colombia News {{!}} Colombia Reports|access-date=2017-04-08|language=en-US}}</ref> The resulting [[Colombian parapolitics scandal|Colombian Parapolitics Scandal]] led to the investigation and arrest of many members of congress, including the former senator and cousin to the president (at the time) [[Mario Uribe Escobar|Mario Uribe]].<ref name=":13">{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2008/apr/23/colombia|title=Colombia's 'parapolitics' scandal casts shadow over president|last=Brodzinsky|first=Sibylla|date=2008-04-23|work=The Guardian|access-date=2017-04-08|language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077}}</ref> With this, despite demobilizing more than 30,000 paramilitary troops and eliciting a peace agreement with some of Colombia's various paramilitary groups through his first term as president,  Uribe came under suspicion because of his cousin's ties to these events. He insisted, however, that he was not tied to these groups.<ref name=":13" />

===Corruption scandals===
Following the 2006 election, Congresswoman Yidis Medina revealed that she had taken bribes in exchange for her vote in favor of the 2004 measure to extend term limits. This event prompted the arrest of Medina, as well as the investigation of other lawmakers, such as [[Sabas Pretelt de la Vega|Sabas Pretelt De La Vega]], and [[Diego Palacio Betancourt]]. It is commonly referred to as the Yidispolitics scandal.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://colombiareports.com/former-ministers-in-court-for-the-yidispolitics-bribery-scandal/|title=Former ministers to be tried for Uribe re-election bribery scandal|date=2012-03-06|work=Colombia News {{!}} Colombia Reports|access-date=2017-04-09|language=en-US}}</ref>

===Changes to term limits===
In 2015, 10 years after the limit on the number of terms served by a president was increased from one to two, congress voted to decrease it back to one. The first vote took place during Uribe's first presidential term (involving the bribing of Congresswoman [[Yidis Medina]]), allowing him to be the first of Colombia's presidents in more than 100 years to run for re-election. His successor (and former defense minister), [[Juan Manuel Santos]], was also able to run and be elected for a second term in 2014, However Santos (in his second term at the time) backed the change, to go into effect once he leaves office. Ex-president Uribe, now a Senator with the Democratic Center party, objected to the reinstatement of the limit, but was outvoted.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/190883/colombia-limits-presidents-to-single-term)|title=Colombia limits presidents to single term|access-date=2017-04-08}}</ref>

===Uribe's career===
Uribe continued to actively participate in politics on a local level after maxing out his time spent as president. He acted as a mentor of sorts to his successor at the presidency, Juan Manuel Santos. However, this bond was broken upon Santos' assumption of the presidency, and the discovery of various political disagreements. Uribe continued to run for offices such as governorships and mayoral positions, representing various conservative political parties in his endeavors. He was unsuccessful in winning these, however. He eventually began an anti-Santos movement, which he called the Democratic Center Party.<ref name=":0" /> Finally, as a representative of the Democratic Center Party, he was able to win a position as a Senator in 2014.<ref name=":14" />

